## Reactive requests

Reactive work begins when a customer reports a fault, loss of service, alarm, leak, unusual noise, comfort complaint or other operational problem.

The initial report may be inaccurate or incomplete. A customer may describe an entire system as failed when one zone is affected, or report an air-conditioning fault that is actually caused by controls, power supply or building conditions.

The incoming request should distinguish:

- who is reporting the issue;
- whether that person can authorise attendance or expenditure;
- affected site, service location, system and asset where known;
- symptom and operational impact;
- immediate safety, environmental or property risk;
- when the issue began;
- actions already taken;
- access availability; and
- applicable contract or charging basis.

Coordinators gather enough information to triage response. Engineers remain responsible for diagnosis within their competence.

## Impact assessment

Assessment considers:

- immediate safety and property risk;
- total or partial service loss;
- critical areas, processes or occupants;
- backup plant and redundancy;
- food, medicine, stock or data exposure;
- weather and expected duration;
- operating hours and business continuity;
- temporary measures already in place; and
- contractual criticality designations.

The same failed asset can produce different priorities at different sites.

## Attendance authority

Before dispatch, Frostline establishes the authority relied upon for the attendance. This may be:

- an included contract entitlement;
- an agreed call-out or diagnostic rate;
- a customer purchase order;
- an accepted quotation;
- a pre-agreed spending limit;
- an internal customer-care or warranty-investigation decision; or
- emergency make-safe authority.

Authority to diagnose does not automatically include authority to repair, order parts, work overtime or appoint a specialist.

Where customer authority cannot be confirmed but immediate risk exists, action is limited to what is reasonably necessary to make safe or prevent escalation, with the decision and follow-up recorded.

## Engineer capability

Allocation considers more than free time. Relevant factors include:

- technical discipline;
- equipment and manufacturer experience;
- current certification and internal authorisation;
- electrical competence;
- refrigerant-handling competence;
- controls knowledge;
- site clearance or induction;
- working-at-height and access capability;
- customer familiarity;
- lone-working suitability;
- supervision requirements; and
- ability to lead multi-person work.

The nearest engineer is not necessarily the correct engineer. Assignment records the basis for material exceptions where a less familiar or more restricted person is used.

## Dispatch acceptance

Receiving an assignment does not require an engineer to accept work silently where:

- the scope is unclear;
- competence or authorisation is insufficient;
- necessary equipment is unavailable;
- safe travel or working time is doubtful;
- access has not been prepared;
- the planned duration is clearly unrealistic; or
- the instruction conflicts with another active responsibility.

Concerns are escalated promptly. Quietly attending and hoping the problem disappears transfers avoidable risk to the customer and business.

## Customer authority and purchase orders

Many customers require a purchase order or other reference before invoicing. The absence of that reference does not always mean Frostline lacked authority to attend, particularly for emergency work, but it can delay or prevent payment.

Staff distinguish between:

- authority to investigate;
- authority to incur a limited call-out charge;
- authority to perform a repair;
- authority to order specified parts;
- authority to proceed up to a spending limit;
- authority to undertake a replacement or project; and
- authority to vary an existing scope.

These permissions may come from different people and have different financial limits.

A purchase-order number quoted in an email is validated against:

- customer entity;
- supplier name;
- scope description;
- value and currency;
- site or project;
- VAT treatment where relevant;
- expiry or release conditions; and
- whether the issuer had authority.

A purchase order does not automatically override Frostline's quotation, accept unreviewed customer terms or authorise work outside the stated value.

## The next-action principle

Every active job, project issue, quotation, purchase requirement, warranty case, complaint or invoice dispute should have:

- one current state;
- one clearly described next material action;
- one accountable owner for that action;
- a due date or review trigger;
- the dependency preventing immediate progress, where applicable; and
- an escalation route if the action cannot be completed.

A list of people copied into a message is not ownership. A generic team queue is useful for intake, but an ageing exception must eventually have a named accountable owner or a deliberately assigned duty role.

## Handoffs

A handoff transfers responsibility for the next action, not merely information.

A complete handoff identifies:

- the receiving owner;
- the outcome required;
- relevant deadline or urgency;
- decisions already made;
- authority already obtained;
- unresolved questions;
- customer commitments made;
- documents and evidence available; and
- what will happen if the receiver does not accept the handoff.

The sender retains responsibility until the receiving person or duty role has accepted the handoff, either explicitly or through a defined operating rule.

## Customer communication

Customers value updates almost as much as attendance. Coordinators communicate:

- acknowledgement and assessed priority;
- the applicable response commitment;
- expected attendance;
- delays or changes;
- engineer findings at an appropriate level of confidence;
- immediate safety and operating status;
- temporary limitations;
- parts, evidence or approval requirements;
- current commercial position where known; and
- next action, owner and review point.

Communication is especially important when a job is waiting. "Open" is not a useful customer explanation. Frostline states what is being awaited, what it is doing next and when the position will be reviewed.

## Communication commitments

For significant incidents, updates occur at the agreed interval even when there is no material technical change.

A useful update states:

- current impact and safety condition;
- latest verified finding;
- what remains uncertain;
- restoration status;
- dependency and owner;
- next action; and
- next update time.

An update saying only “still chasing” provides little control value unless it identifies what is being chased and what happens next.

## Contract hierarchy

Where SLA wording conflicts with the signed contract, accepted quotation, schedule of service or formally agreed variation, the applicable contractual hierarchy is reviewed rather than silently selecting the most convenient interpretation.

Operational teams should know:

- which document defines the target;
- which customer, sites and assets are covered;
- effective dates and renewal position;
- coverage hours;
- exclusions;
- service-credit rules; and
- who can approve a change.

Informal promises by an engineer or coordinator do not amend an SLA unless made by an authorised person and properly recorded.

### Customer-scope decisions

Customer authority establishes permission to perform work and, where applicable, responsibility for payment.

Acceptable evidence may include:

- an accepted quotation;
- a purchase order;
- a signed contract;
- a contractually agreed call-out or repair limit;
- a written variation instruction;
- recorded verbal authority from a known authorised contact; or
- an emergency instruction supported by the applicable contract and escalation record.

Site presence, urgency or an engineer's recommendation do not by themselves establish customer authority.

## Readiness checks

Before work is treated as ready, planning considers whether the following are sufficiently established:

- customer, site, system or asset identity;
- reported need or accepted scope;
- applicable contract or quotation;
- customer authority and any spending limit;
- Frostline authority to commit labour, materials or subcontractors;
- priority and response commitment;
- access, inductions, permits and shutdown arrangements;
- known hazards and method requirements;
- engineer competence and authorisation;
- parts, tools, test equipment and specialist resources;
- realistic duration;
- customer communication; and
- dependencies whose failure would make attendance unproductive.

Not every item must be perfect before an emergency diagnostic attendance. The planner should, however, know which uncertainties are deliberately being accepted.

## Financial record boundaries

Frostline distinguishes:

- customer account;
- contracting legal entity;
- site and operational contact;
- quotation or contract;
- customer purchase order;
- job or project;
- attendance;
- supplier purchase order;
- supplier invoice;
- customer invoice;
- credit note;
- warranty claim or recovery; and
- payment.

These records may be linked many-to-many. One customer invoice may cover several jobs. One job may use several supplier invoices. One warranty recovery may relate to a failed component originally purchased for a project and replaced on a later remedial job.

Using one reference field to stand in for all of these relationships creates fragile accounting and poor auditability.

## Testing and recommissioning

After intervention, the engineer tests enough to support the claimed outcome. Tests may include:

- pressure, vacuum or leak checks;
- electrical measurements;
- temperatures and pressures;
- control sequence and demand;
- alarms and safeties;
- drainage;
- airflow or water flow;
- combustion or emissions where applicable;
- configuration review; and
- observation through a suitable cycle or load condition.

The record states test conditions and limitations. A unit starting briefly does not prove durable repair or design performance.

## Operational completion and financial closure

Frostline distinguishes:

- attendance complete;
- authorised job scope complete;
- project practically complete;
- customer issue resolved;
- invoiceable event achieved;
- invoice issued;
- invoice paid;
- cost and recovery finalised; and
- job or project financially closed.

These states may occur at different times. A job may be operationally complete but held from invoicing because authority or evidence is incomplete. A staged project invoice may be issued before practical completion. A warranty repair may be complete while manufacturer recovery remains outstanding.

No single generic "closed" status should conceal those differences.

## Materials and stock cost

Materials may be:

- purchased directly for a job or project;
- issued from warehouse stock;
- used from van stock;
- returned unused;
- transferred between jobs;
- scrapped;
- replaced under warranty; or
- retained as project surplus.

The financial record should preserve quantity, cost basis, source, destination and relevant return or recovery.

Unused project materials do not disappear from cost merely because they remain in a van or depot. They are returned to controlled stock, retained against a named future requirement or written off under authority.

## Invoice triggers

Invoices may be raised on:

- completion of a chargeable attendance;
- completion of an approved repair;
- delivery of a maintenance period or scheduled attendance group;
- delivery of equipment or materials where contractually agreed;
- project deposit or milestone;
- application for payment;
- practical completion;
- approved variation;
- cancellation or aborted attendance under agreed terms; or
- another defined contractual event.

The invoice trigger must identify both the commercial entitlement and the evidence that the event occurred.

Completion in the engineer diary is not automatically sufficient. Finance may require:

- accepted quotation, contract or rate basis;
- customer purchase order or recorded exception;
- attendance and labour evidence;
- materials and subcontract detail;
- completion or commissioning evidence;
- variation approval;
- customer acknowledgement where required; and
- manager review of unusual or disputed treatment.

## Invoice validation and release

Before release, finance checks evidence proportionate to value and risk. Typical checks include:

- correct customer legal entity;
- billing address, portal and purchase-order requirements;
- invoice trigger achieved;
- scope and charging basis;
- labour, material and subcontract completeness;
- VAT treatment;
- approved variations;
- warranty or goodwill status;
- previous deposits, applications or invoices;
- retention and deductions; and
- known disputes.

An invoice may be held where evidence is incomplete, but the hold itself must have a reason, owner and target action. "Waiting for paperwork" is insufficient without identifying what is missing and who must provide it.

## Sign-off

Customer acknowledgement confirms attendance and receipt of the recorded outcome. It does not automatically:

- approve unquoted charges;
- accept technical design;
- waive defects;
- establish warranty responsibility; or
- confirm final job closure.

Where no authorised contact is available, alternative evidence and communication are recorded.

## Customer handover

Before leaving, the engineer explains in proportionate language:

- what was found;
- what was done;
- current service and safety condition;
- whether the measure is temporary;
- any operating restriction;
- what remains unresolved;
- whether further work requires quotation or approval; and
- who will contact the customer next.

The engineer avoids definitive liability or warranty statements unless that decision has been authorised.

## Authority to proceed

Where diagnosis identifies work beyond the current scope, the engineer separates:

- technical recommendation;
- customer authority;
- Frostline expenditure authority;
- availability of parts or resources; and
- decision to perform work now or create follow-on work.

Material verbal approval records the person, time, scope and limit. The engineer does not promise price, programme, warranty acceptance or replacement unless authorised to do so.

## Maintenance completion states

A maintenance attendance may be:

- complete;
- partially complete;
- unable to complete because of access;
- unable to complete because of safety;
- unable to complete because the asset or system could not be identified;
- unable to complete because prerequisite customer action was absent; or
- complete with defects or recommendations requiring separate follow-on action.

The maintenance job or contractual obligation must not be marked complete merely because an engineer attended site. Completion requires the authorised task set and evidence, or an explicit exception decision.

## Defects found during maintenance

Engineers distinguish between maintenance completed within the authorised attendance and defects requiring further action.

A defect may lead to:

- an immediate minor repair within both customer and Frostline authority;
- make-safe action;
- isolation for safety;
- a return attendance under existing authority;
- a repair quotation;
- a replacement recommendation;
- specialist investigation;
- a suspected warranty case; or
- monitored deterioration with an agreed review point.

Finding a defect is not authority to repair it. The engineer records the condition, consequence, evidence, technical recommendation and immediate safety status. Operations then establishes whether the next action is already covered, separately chargeable, subject to quotation or dependent on another party.

Vague notes such as "unit faulty" are not sufficient when a technical, safety or commercial decision is required.

## Customer access and site readiness

A confirmed appointment can fail if access dependencies are not prepared. Planning records relevant:

- opening hours and contacts;
- keys, codes, escorts and security clearance;
- inductions and permits;
- tenant or occupant notice;
- asbestos and hazard information;
- shutdown permission;
- isolation ownership;
- loading, parking and delivery access; and
- restricted work periods.

Where the customer owns an access action, Frostline still owns the decision to chase, proceed provisionally or reschedule.

## Maintenance obligations and schedules

Maintenance can be scheduled annually, six-monthly, quarterly or at another agreed frequency. Different assets at the same site may require different frequencies, task sets or evidence.

The company distinguishes:

- contractual maintenance obligations;
- statutory or certification-related activities;
- manufacturer-recommended maintenance;
- customer-requested additional tasks; and
- advisory activities that are not part of the current contract.

Coordinators try to group work efficiently by site and geography without allowing contractual dates to drift. School holidays, retail peaks, hospitality occupancy, tenant access and plant shutdown windows can constrain attendance.

A missed attendance does not disappear. The associated obligation remains active until it is completed, formally rescheduled, varied, cancelled under authority or recorded as no longer recoverable with the consequences understood. Where a contract defines SLA targets for maintenance delivery (such as completing all quarterly visits within the quarter), the missed-maintenance obligation and any resulting SLA breach are recorded against the contractual period in which they were due, not the period in which they are eventually completed.

## Reactive and service invoicing

Reactive work commonly combines call-out, labour, travel, parts, access equipment and subcontract cost. Contract cover may remove, cap or alter some charges.

A single reported fault can create several attendances, purchases and decisions. Frostline may:

- invoice the diagnostic attendance separately;
- invoice authorised temporary or make-safe work;
- quote follow-on repair;
- invoice each stage under schedule-of-rates terms;
- consolidate after resolution; or
- hold customer charging pending a suspected warranty decision.

The chosen treatment follows the contract, authority and customer communication. It must not be inferred solely from the job description.

Maintenance contracts may be billed annually, quarterly, monthly or against visit completion. Billing timing does not necessarily match the physical delivery pattern. Deferred or missed maintenance obligations remain visible even where periodic billing has already occurred.

## Priority and urgency

Frostline considers several factors when prioritising reactive work:

- risk to people or property;
- complete or partial loss of critical service;
- site type and operating hours;
- vulnerable occupants;
- food, stock, process or technology risk;
- contractual response commitment;
- availability of safe temporary measures;
- customer relationship;
- engineer travel and capability; and
- the effect of displacing already-promised work.

Priority is a decision, not simply a label supplied by the requester. It is not determined only by who calls most loudly. A loss of cooling in a server room or refrigeration failure may outrank a comfort complaint even when the latter customer is more persistent.

Where reprioritisation threatens another contractual commitment, the displaced work receives its own owner, communication and recovery plan.

## Asset schedules

The asset schedule identifies the equipment covered by the agreement. It may record:

- site and location;
- asset identifier;
- equipment type;
- manufacturer and model;
- serial number;
- duty or served area;
- refrigerant where applicable;
- maintenance frequency;
- known condition; and
- specific exclusions or limitations.

An asset not listed is not automatically covered merely because it is physically present at the site. Equally, Frostline should not knowingly continue charging for equipment that has been removed or permanently decommissioned.

Asset schedules are reviewed when equipment is added, replaced, relocated or removed. Material changes may require a contract variation and repricing.

## Replacement and retirement

When equipment is replaced, the old record remains part of the site's history. The replacement should be distinguishable from the removed equipment even when it serves the same location and purpose.

Retired equipment may still matter for:

- historic invoices and certificates;
- warranty or dispute evidence;
- refrigerant records;
- project costing;
- recurring site problems; and
- understanding changes to system configuration.

## Review and renewal

Contracts are reviewed before renewal. The review considers:

- asset changes;
- maintenance completion;
- reactive demand;
- parts and subcontract cost;
- response performance;
- recurring defects;
- customer feedback;
- equipment condition;
- price adequacy; and
- any changes in law, standards or manufacturer requirements.

Renewal is not purely an inflation exercise. A contract may need redesign where the service pattern or risk has materially changed.

## Coverage hours

The agreement states:

- time zone;
- covered days and hours;
- public-holiday treatment;
- seasonal arrangements;
- whether clocks run continuously or only in covered time;
- service channels valid out of hours; and
- whether remote response and attendance have different coverage.

A four-working-hour target is materially different from four elapsed hours. Reporting preserves that distinction.

## Attendance targets

An attendance target defines:

- start event;
- target duration;
- priority;
- coverage calendar;
- geographic scope;
- required engineer suitability;
- permitted clock stops;
- evidence of arrival; and
- treatment of no access or incorrect site information.

A person reaching the car park does not necessarily constitute meaningful attendance where the agreed service location remains inaccessible. Equally, customer delay after a documented arrival may trigger a permitted clock stop.

## Clock stops

A clock stop is permitted only where the contract allows it and the dependency genuinely prevents the measured action.

Common reasons include:

- no access;
- absent permit or induction;
- customer-directed delay;
- waiting for customer authority;
- unsafe conditions outside Frostline's control;
- unavailable utilities;
- third-party work;
- specialist access arrangements;
- manufacturer decision; or
- part availability.

The record includes reason, start time, evidence, expected actor, Frostline owner, chase or review point and restart event.

Clock stops must not be added retrospectively merely to improve attainment.

## Restoration and resolution

Restoration is defined in customer-relevant terms, such as minimum temperature control, operation of one duty unit, safe restricted use or temporary equipment.

A temporary restoration records:

- achieved service level;
- operating limits;
- residual risk;
- expiry or review trigger;
- customer understanding; and
- owner of permanent resolution.

Resolution targets are used only where Frostline can reasonably control the dependencies. Where parts, third parties or customer decisions dominate elapsed time, the contract may instead require diagnosis, action planning, escalation and scheduled updates.

## Temporary measures

Temporary repairs, overrides, loan equipment and partial-service arrangements create a mandatory future action.

The record should state:

- what temporary measure was introduced;
- why it was necessary;
- operational limits;
- residual risk;
- who approved it;
- customer understanding;
- inspection or expiry date; and
- permanent resolution owner.

A temporary measure without an expiry or review trigger is an undocumented permanent change waiting to cause trouble.

## Multi-person and supervised work

Where work requires several people, all essential roles are planned together. The plan identifies:

- lead person;
- required competence mix;
- supervision arrangement;
- simultaneous or staggered attendance;
- shared tools and access equipment;
- safe lifting or isolation needs; and
- consequence if one participant becomes unavailable.

Splitting a planned team to absorb reactive demand requires an explicit judgement that the remaining method stays safe and viable.

## Emergency displacement

When urgent work displaces existing commitments, the decision record should identify:

- why displacement was justified;
- who authorised material reprioritisation;
- which work was affected;
- customer communication required;
- new owner and next action for displaced work; and
- whether repeated displacement indicates a capacity or contract problem.

The latest loud request should not silently become the highest priority.

## Dynamic rescheduling

The schedule is reviewed when:

- an emergency arrives;
- diagnosis changes the required competence or duration;
- an attendance completes early or overruns;
- an engineer becomes unavailable;
- access fails;
- materials are delayed or found unsuitable;
- weather prevents safe work;
- a customer cancels or reprioritises;
- another trade or utility fails to perform; or
- a temporary measure approaches its review point.

Rescheduling is a decision with consequences, not drag-and-drop administration. The coordinator assesses downstream customer commitments, contractual risk, project disruption, travel, fatigue and whether the displaced work has a new owner and date.

## Scheduling objectives

A controlled schedule aims to:

- protect people, property and critical operations;
- meet contractual response and maintenance commitments;
- assign people who are competent and authorised for the work;
- reduce avoidable travel and mobilisation;
- complete planned work within agreed windows;
- preserve resilience for reactive demand;
- avoid unsafe fatigue and unrealistic workload;
- protect project continuity and customer promises; and
- make dependencies and uncertainty visible.

These objectives compete. A diary filled to theoretical capacity may appear efficient while being incapable of absorbing an emergency, overrun or sickness absence.

## Customer stop and continued work

Frostline may restrict further non-essential work for customers materially outside terms. The decision considers:

- debt age and value;
- dispute credibility;
- current exposure;
- critical site or safety implications;
- existing contractual obligations;
- deposits or security;
- customer communication; and
- likelihood of recovery.

Finance controls credit status, but operational and safety implications are escalated rather than handled by a rigid automatic rule.

Emergency make-safe work may proceed under separate authority without implying that wider service has been released or that old debt is waived.

## Emergency and make-safe work

Emergency action may proceed where needed to control immediate risk. It should be limited to the reasonable make-safe or damage-prevention outcome.

The engineer records:

- condition encountered;
- immediate risk;
- action taken;
- equipment state left;
- customer informed;
- authority relied upon;
- cost or materials committed; and
- mandatory follow-up.

Make-safe is not restoration, permanent repair or job closure.

## Emergency authority

Emergency authority exists to protect people, property, critical operations and equipment where delay would materially worsen the outcome.

Emergency action should be limited to what is reasonably necessary to make safe, prevent escalation or provide a controlled temporary measure. It is not a blanket authority to complete an entire repair or replacement without commercial review.

The decision record should capture:

- the condition encountered;
- the immediate risk;
- alternatives considered;
- who was contacted;
- what authority was relied upon;
- expenditure or commitment made;
- temporary limitations; and
- required follow-up.

## Purchase authority

Approval limits consider value and risk. Additional review may be required for:

- deposits or prepayment;
- non-returnable or made-to-order goods;
- foreign currency;
- unusual supplier terms;
- new or unapproved supplier;
- long lead time;
- uncertain customer recovery;
- low-margin quoted work;
- safety-critical substitution;
- warranty cost awaiting recovery; or
- cumulative exposure across several related orders.

Splitting orders to avoid an approval threshold is prohibited.

## Acceptance validation

Before treating a quotation as accepted, Frostline checks:

- the accepting party is identifiable and has suitable authority;
- the accepted quotation number and revision are clear;
- selected options are unambiguous;
- the purchase order or acceptance value matches the offer;
- customer terms do not introduce unreviewed conditions;
- requested dates are still achievable;
- quotation validity and supplier pricing remain usable;
- credit or deposit requirements are satisfied or explicitly waived; and
- qualifications, assumptions and exclusions have not been contradicted.

A purchase order with a familiar number is not sufficient if its scope, value, legal entity or terms do not match the quotation.

## Options and alternatives

Where useful, Frostline may present:

- a compliant base solution;
- higher-efficiency equipment;
- enhanced controls;
- alternative manufacturers;
- phased replacement;
- maintenance options; or
- separately priced remedial work.

Options should be genuinely comparable and clearly identify any change in performance or scope.

The customer's acceptance must identify which option or combination is authorised. Acceptance of one option does not authorise omitted enhancements or assumptions from another.

## Identity and duplicate records

Records that appear similar must not be merged solely on name, address or description. Frostline may encounter:

- trading names differing from legal entities;
- multiple customers at one site;
- one customer with several billing accounts;
- replaced assets retaining old labels;
- duplicate serial numbers entered incorrectly;
- several jobs concerning the same symptom; and
- contacts moving between organisations.

Reconciliation should preserve source evidence, explain the chosen relationship and retain aliases where they are operationally useful.

## Internal approval

Higher-value, lower-margin or higher-risk quotations receive additional review. Approval may consider technical solution, supplier evidence, labour allowance, contractual risk, cash flow, warranty exposure and delivery capacity.

Internal approval should identify:

- the quotation revision approved;
- the proposed price and expected margin;
- material qualifications or departures;
- approval conditions;
- delegated authority relied upon; and
- any matter requiring director, finance, technical or legal review.

Approval to issue is not approval to purchase equipment or commence delivery. Those commitments arise only after valid customer authority, credit acceptance where required and operational release.

## Commercial release

Customer acceptance is necessary but may not be sufficient for Frostline to commit cost.

Before significant procurement or mobilisation, the appropriate owner confirms:

- accepted scope and revision;
- valid customer authority;
- credit or deposit position;
- Frostline delegated approval;
- expected margin and risk;
- supplier or subcontract commitment required;
- invoicing route and evidence requirements; and
- material conditions still outstanding.

Release may be limited to a defined purchase, survey, mobilisation stage or value. Conditional release is recorded with the condition, owner and review point.

## Commercial release to delivery

Award creates a controlled transition from opportunity to authorised work. Frostline records:

- the customer authority relied upon;
- the internally approved quotation revision;
- the job or project created;
- budget ownership;
- required deposits or credit conditions;
- permitted purchasing commitments;
- promised milestones;
- unresolved dependencies; and
- the person owning the next action.

Where acceptance is incomplete or conditional, the work remains awaiting commercial release rather than being described vaguely as “won” or “live”.

## Handover from sales and estimating

Sales and estimating transfer the accepted commercial basis to installation operations. A complete handoff identifies:

- customer and site contacts and their authority;
- quotation number, revision and accepted value;
- inclusions, exclusions and assumptions;
- equipment and principal materials;
- estimate maturity and unresolved uncertainty;
- design and survey information;
- promised, requested and provisional dates;
- access and working restrictions;
- subcontracted elements;
- payment terms, deposit and credit conditions;
- warranty and maintenance commitments;
- known technical and commercial risks;
- customer responsibilities; and
- documents or decisions still outstanding.

The estimator retains responsibility for the handoff until the receiving project owner accepts it or explicitly rejects it because information, authority or feasibility is inadequate.

The estimator's understanding often contains context not obvious from the quotation alone. Complex or unusual projects therefore require a structured conversation and evidence transfer, not merely forwarding a PDF.

## Estimate maturity

Frostline distinguishes between different levels of cost and price confidence:

- **budget indication** — an early range used for feasibility or customer planning;
- **concept estimate** — based on a proposed solution but still subject to survey, design or supplier confirmation;
- **developed estimate** — supported by a sufficiently defined scope, measured quantities, labour plan and current supplier evidence;
- **approved quotation basis** — internally reviewed and suitable to issue as a formal offer; and
- **delivery forecast** — the current expected cost after award, incorporating approved changes and known delivery information.

These are related records but not interchangeable. A budget indication must not silently become a fixed-price commitment, and a delivery forecast must not rewrite the accepted quotation history.

## Site survey

The estimator gathers enough information to understand existing conditions. A survey may record:

- site layout and access;
- existing plant and controls;
- model and serial details;
- served areas and operating requirements;
- electrical supplies and isolators;
- pipework, ductwork and drainage routes;
- lifting and working-at-height requirements;
- fire stopping and builders' work;
- asbestos information;
- working-hour restrictions;
- noise or planning constraints; and
- commissioning and handover expectations.

Photographs, sketches and measurements support the written survey but do not replace clear notes.

Where information remains unverified, the estimate records the uncertainty and how it is treated: further survey, customer dependency, provisional allowance, exclusion, option or explicit risk acceptance. “Site to be confirmed” is not a sufficient control for a material unknown.

## Pricing approaches

Frostline uses several pricing approaches:

- fixed-price quotation;
- schedule of rates;
- time and materials;
- contract price;
- capped diagnostic or investigation charge;
- staged project valuation; and
- negotiated variation.

The company does not use one universal margin percentage. Commercial judgement reflects technical risk, customer relationship, competition, workload, payment terms and confidence in the scope.

## Quotation content

A formal quotation normally includes:

- customer and site;
- quotation number and revision;
- scope of works;
- equipment and principal materials;
- programme or lead-time statement;
- price and VAT;
- payment terms;
- validity period;
- assumptions and exclusions;
- warranty position;
- customer responsibilities; and
- acceptance method.

Where relevant it also identifies key systems and assets, expected outage, design responsibility, provisional sums, options, required access, dependencies, deliverables and the boundary between making safe, temporary restoration and permanent completion.

A provisional sum within an otherwise fixed-price quotation means the stated value is an allowance subject to adjustment once the identified uncertainty is resolved. The quotation states what triggers the adjustment, how the final value will be determined and whether customer approval is required before the provisional element proceeds. A provisional sum is not an invitation for Frostline to incur any cost and recover it later without agreement.

## Assumptions

Assumptions make the basis of price visible. Examples include:

- work during normal hours;
- unrestricted access to agreed areas;
- suitable existing electrical supply;
- structurally adequate mounting surfaces;
- no asbestos disturbance;
- customer-provided shutdown approval;
- clear routes for pipework or ductwork; and
- use of specified equipment remaining available.

An assumption should be realistic and relevant. It should not quietly transfer every foreseeable risk to the customer.

Each material assumption should be testable. Where failure of an assumption would change price, programme or method, the quotation states the consequence or variation route rather than leaving it implicit.

## Materials and bought-in services

Material estimates may include equipment, pipework, ductwork, insulation, controls, electrical components, fixings, supports, consumables, refrigerant, filters and disposal materials.

Bought-in services may include:

- crane or lifting operations;
- scaffolding or powered access;
- electrical work;
- roofing or builders' work;
- asbestos surveys or removal;
- fire stopping;
- specialist commissioning;
- controls integration; and
- waste disposal.

Supplier prices, freight, lead times and validity dates are checked where they could affect the offer.

A supplier quotation is evidence of likely cost, not authority to purchase. Frostline purchase commitments follow the separate approval and purchasing controls defined in the governance and supply-chain references.

## Project release states

Frostline distinguishes the following states:

- **accepted, not reviewed** — customer acceptance received but internal validation not complete;
- **commercially held** — acceptance exists but deposit, credit, order wording, margin or contractual issue prevents commitment;
- **technically held** — design, survey, selection or responsibility issue prevents release;
- **operationally held** — labour, access, programme, permits or dependencies are not ready;
- **released for procurement** — approved commitments may be placed within defined limits;
- **released for mobilisation** — site work may commence against the agreed plan;
- **in delivery** — authorised work is being executed;
- **partially complete** — defined scope remains outstanding;
- **practically complete** — intended operational outcome is available subject to recorded residual items;
- **commercially unresolved** — variation, valuation, retention, claim or invoice remains open; and
- **closed** — operational, documentary and commercial closure criteria are satisfied or remaining obligations have been explicitly transferred.

These states may overlap in controlled ways. For example, procurement may be released before mobilisation, and practical completion may precede final commercial closure.

## Purchase orders

A purchase order records the agreed commitment and normally includes:

- supplier;
- delivery and invoice addresses;
- job, project or stock reference;
- item, quantity and specification;
- agreed price and VAT basis;
- carriage;
- required or confirmed date;
- delivery contact;
- technical conditions;
- documentation or certification requirements;
- warranty and return references where relevant; and
- authorising person.

A verbal emergency order is documented promptly with the same essential evidence.

## Supplier acknowledgement

Critical orders are not treated as secure until the supplier confirms:

- accepted specification;
- quantity;
- price;
- expected dispatch or delivery;
- any back-order;
- substitutions;
- cancellation status; and
- special conditions.

Silence after issuing a purchase order is not positive confirmation.

## Internal release decision

Before significant commitment, the project owner confirms:

- the accepted scope is identifiable and internally understood;
- quotation assumptions remain valid or have been resolved;
- the customer's order does not introduce unreviewed terms or scope;
- required deposit, credit and commercial approvals exist;
- design responsibility and deliverables are clear;
- major equipment selection is technically supported;
- procurement authority and budget exist;
- proposed dates are achievable enough to communicate;
- critical customer and third-party actions are owned; and
- unacceptable risks have been escalated.

Release may be conditional. Conditions must be explicit, owned and time-bound. A vague instruction to "get started while we sort the paperwork" is not a controlled release.

## Pre-start planning

Before mobilisation, Frostline confirms preparation proportionate to the risk and complexity of the project. This may include:

- final survey or validation attendance;
- design information, calculations and equipment selection;
- drawings, schematics or marked-up plans;
- method statements and risk assessments;
- site induction and permit arrangements;
- electrical, structural or builders' work requirements;
- lifting plans, access equipment or scaffolding;
- isolation and shutdown windows;
- delivery, unloading and storage arrangements;
- waste and refrigerant handling;
- subcontractor appointment and scope;
- customer decisions and enabling work;
- interface responsibilities with other trades;
- commissioning and handover expectations; and
- contingency for foreseeable disruption.

A start date remains provisional until the dependencies necessary for that commitment are confirmed.

## Design and responsibility boundaries

Projects may involve design by Frostline, a manufacturer, a consultant, the customer or another contractor. The project record identifies:

- who owns the design basis;
- what Frostline has verified;
- what information Frostline is entitled to rely upon;
- interfaces requiring coordination;
- review and approval responsibilities;
- design changes and their authority; and
- limitations that remain with another party.

Approval of a drawing does not automatically transfer all design responsibility to the approver. Equally, Frostline must not install an obviously unsuitable or unsafe design merely because another party produced it.

## Materials and equipment

Major equipment is commonly ordered specifically for the project. Ancillaries may come from stock, local suppliers or staged project orders.

The team controls more than an ordered/not-ordered status. It needs to know:

- approved specification and revision;
- supplier and purchase authority;
- acknowledgement and promised date;
- actual delivery status;
- location and custody;
- completeness;
- damage or discrepancy;
- allocation to the project; and
- return, warranty or storage constraints.

Shortages, damage or incorrect equipment can affect several linked activities. Substitution requires technical approval, customer or consultant approval where applicable, and commercial approval where price, warranty, performance or programme changes.

## Programme and resource planning

Projects compete with service operations for people, vehicles and specialist skills. Frostline aims to keep an installation team together where practical, but engineers may move between projects to provide commissioning, welding, controls or supervisory capability.

The programme distinguishes:

- elapsed dates;
- engineer-days or trade effort;
- customer and third-party dependencies;
- equipment and material lead times;
- hold points;
- commissioning windows;
- float and contingency; and
- dates formally promised to the customer.

A project needing ten engineer-days may span several weeks because of phased access, equipment delivery, other trades or customer shutdown periods.

Changes to one project must consider the work displaced elsewhere. Reallocation without an owner for the affected commitment merely moves the failure.

## Decision evidence

Material decisions should preserve:

- the decision made;
- the decision-maker;
- date and time;
- evidence considered;
- assumptions and uncertainties;
- limits or conditions;
- affected customer, site, asset, job, quote, order or invoice;
- follow-up owner; and
- superseding decision where one later changes the position.

An approval is not merely a status label. It is a traceable business event with scope and limits.

## Project creation and identity

A project record is created when the work requires coordinated control of outcome, programme, budget, resources and dependencies beyond a routine service job.

The project should identify:

- customer and contracting entity;
- site or sites;
- accepted quotation, contract or order;
- project owner;
- technical or design owner where applicable;
- commercial owner;
- authorised value and current forecast;
- required outcome;
- key milestones;
- major dependencies; and
- current release state.

A customer purchase order, supplier order and project identifier are related but distinct records. None should be used as a substitute for another merely because their references are familiar.

## Completion states

Frostline distinguishes:

- **operationally available** — the relevant service can be used, possibly with limitations;
- **installation complete** — authorised physical installation scope is complete;
- **commissioned** — defined testing and functional verification are complete;
- **practically complete** — intended outcome is available with only recorded minor residual work;
- **documentation complete** — required handover records are delivered;
- **financially complete** — applications, invoices, variations, credits and retention treatment are resolved as far as due;
- **warranty registered** — required manufacturer registration is accepted; and
- **project closed** — all required closure conditions are met or residual obligations have been explicitly transferred and remain controlled elsewhere.

One state must not be used as evidence of another without justification.

## Delegated limits

Frostline uses role-based delegated limits rather than assuming that job title alone grants unrestricted authority.

A delegated limit may be constrained by:

- financial value;
- customer or contract;
- work type;
- urgency;
- margin;
- supplier;
- duration;
- legal or safety exposure; and
- whether the commitment is reversible.

Delegation may be temporary, suspended or narrowed. Absence of the usual approver does not automatically pass their authority to the next available person.

## Change classification

Not every departure from plan is a customer variation. A discovered issue is classified before commercial treatment is assumed.

Possible classifications include:

- customer-requested scope change;
- consultant or design change;
- unforeseeable site condition;
- customer or third-party failure to meet a responsibility;
- supplier or manufacturer issue;
- Frostline estimating omission;
- Frostline design or workmanship correction;
- clarification within existing scope;
- acceleration or changed working method; or
- emergency make-safe action.

Classification may remain provisional while evidence is gathered. The project owner preserves chronology and cost without forcing an early conclusion merely to keep work moving.

## Variations and scope authority

A variation is a documented change to authorised scope, price, programme or responsibility. Discovery or discussion alone is not approval.

A variation record should identify:

- original accepted basis;
- requested or discovered change;
- reason and classification;
- technical consequence;
- price or valuation basis;
- programme consequence;
- assumptions and exclusions;
- customer decision-maker;
- Frostline approver;
- date and form of authority; and
- effect on procurement and site instructions.

Engineers should not casually promise additional work. Where immediate action is necessary to protect safety, property or completed work, emergency authority is limited to that protective action and the commercial position is escalated separately.

Not every complication is recoverable from the customer. Frostline remains responsible for matters that should reasonably have been included, were caused by its own error or fall within an accepted risk.

## Progress and commercial control

Longer projects may be invoiced through deposits, staged payments, applications for payment or completion invoices. Operational progress and financial progress are related but not identical.

A project can be physically advanced but commercially blocked by missing evidence. It can also be invoiced ahead of final completion where the contract permits staged billing.

Managers monitor:

- authorised value;
- pending and approved variations;
- committed purchase cost;
- actual labour and subcontract cost;
- expected final cost;
- application, certification, invoice and cash status;
- retention;
- warranty exposure; and
- expected final margin.

Forecast deterioration is made visible when identified. It is not deferred until completion simply because the final value remains uncertain.

## Changes after award

New information after award is classified before action:

- clarification within the accepted scope;
- correction of Frostline's own error;
- customer-requested change;
- site condition differing from an explicit assumption;
- omission or dependency belonging to another party;
- emergency make-safe work; or
- separately proposed follow-on work.

The classification affects technical response, customer communication, cost responsibility and whether a variation is required. Discovery alone does not constitute approval.

## Handover

Handover evidence varies by project but may include:

- equipment schedules and serial numbers;
- commissioning sheets;
- test results and certificates;
- photographs;
- manufacturer documentation;
- warranty registration and conditions;
- operating guidance;
- maintenance recommendations and contract implications;
- drawings or marked-up records;
- training provided;
- keys, passwords or configuration information under controlled handling; and
- outstanding, temporary or excluded items.

The recipient of technical handover may differ from the customer contact who authorised or pays for the work. Acceptance of technical information does not necessarily constitute commercial acceptance or waive defects.

## Warranty registration

Many equipment warranties depend on timely and accurate registration. Required information may include:

- installer details;
- installation and commissioning date;
- site and customer;
- model and serial numbers;
- commissioning readings;
- proof of purchase;
- service history; and
- evidence of approved installation.

Frostline distinguishes:

- registration required;
- registration submitted;
- registration accepted;
- evidence incomplete;
- registration rejected; and
- warranty conditions still requiring future maintenance or inspection.

Submission is not the same as acceptance. Late or incomplete registration can weaken a valid claim. Frostline treats registration as a controlled handover activity rather than optional administration.

## Defects, snags and incomplete work

A defect is work that does not meet the required standard. A snag may be a minor defect or incomplete item identified near handover. An excluded item is not a snag merely because the customer expected it.

Each residual item should identify:

- classification;
- location and affected asset or system;
- required outcome;
- responsible party;
- target date;
- access or dependency;
- commercial treatment where relevant; and
- evidence required for closure.

The project should not be described as "nearly finished" without a defined residual list and owners.

## Project closure review

Before closure, the project owner confirms:

- authorised scope and approved variations are accounted for;
- residual items are complete or transferred to named owners;
- commissioning and handover evidence is retained;
- asset and warranty records are updated;
- supplier and subcontract issues are resolved or controlled;
- final cost and value are credible;
- invoicing and retention positions are understood;
- customer commitments are complete;
- temporary measures have permanent owners; and
- learning actions are recorded where material.

Closure is a business decision supported by evidence, not merely removal from the active-project list.

## Project invoicing

Installation projects may use deposits, staged invoices, applications for payment, completion invoices and retention. The commercial basis is inherited from the accepted quotation or contract and any approved variations.

Frostline distinguishes:

- value applied for;
- value assessed or certified;
- value invoiced;
- value paid;
- retention withheld;
- variations pending approval;
- contra-charges or deductions; and
- final account position.

The difference between applied, certified, invoiced and paid values remains visible until resolved.

Where retention is withheld pending a defects or warranty period, the retention release is a distinct financial event from warranty-case closure. Retention may be released once the contractual defects period expires without outstanding notified defects, even if a separate warranty recovery against a manufacturer remains in progress. Conversely, a warranty case closed in Frostline's favour does not automatically trigger retention release if other contractual conditions remain unsatisfied. Each record has its own owner and review date.

Variations need enough contemporaneous evidence to support entitlement and value. Work performed without timely commercial notice may be technically valid but difficult to recover.

A disputed valuation is an active exception with an owner, evidence requirement, next action and escalation point.

## Financial closure

A job or project is financially closed when:

- all material labour, materials and subcontract costs are recorded or deliberately accrued;
- invoicing entitlement has been exercised or consciously waived under authority;
- customer credits and disputes are resolved or transferred to controlled cases;
- supplier credits and returns are processed or written off;
- warranty recoveries are received, rejected or closed under authority;
- final value and expected margin are credible; and
- no ownerless financial action remains.

Financial closure does not erase future warranty obligations, retention or latent disputes. Those remain linked records with their own owners and review dates.

## Warranty cost and recovery

Warranty treatment follows [Warranty responsibility and remedial work](../warranty/warranty-responsibility-and-remedial-work.md).

Frostline records separately:

- remedial labour and travel;
- parts, refrigerant and consumables;
- access, freight and subcontract cost;
- customer invoice or agreed charge;
- customer credit or goodwill value;
- manufacturer or supplier claim;
- subcontract recovery;
- value approved;
- value actually received; and
- final cost absorbed by Frostline.

Zero-price customer work is not zero-cost work. A promised manufacturer credit is not recovered value until confirmed and processed.

The remedial job may close operationally while the warranty case remains financially open. Conversely, a claim credit may arrive after the original project has otherwise closed and must still be attributed to the correct case and reporting period.

## Identity

An equipment record is most useful when it can be matched confidently to the physical item on site. Identification may use:

- manufacturer and model;
- serial number;
- customer asset tag;
- Frostline reference;
- plant-room or room location;
- system designation;
- indoor-to-outdoor unit relationship; and
- photographs or label details.

In older buildings, labels may be missing, duplicated, inaccessible or inaccurate. Engineers sometimes have to identify equipment by location and description until better evidence is available.

## Commercial relationships

A single organisation can have more than one commercial arrangement with Frostline. For example, a hotel group may hold a maintenance contract for twelve sites while also commissioning a separate replacement project at one hotel.

Different people within the same customer organisation may perform different roles:

- commercial or procurement contacts approve suppliers and prices;
- facilities contacts report faults and coordinate access;
- site managers confirm local arrangements;
- finance contacts receive invoices and statements;
- technical contacts review designs or compliance evidence; and
- senior sponsors handle escalations and relationship matters.

The person who reports a fault may not be authorised to approve expenditure. The person who approves expenditure may never visit the affected site.

## Service and work history

Equipment history can include:

- planned-maintenance visits;
- faults and reactive attendances;
- parts replaced;
- refrigerant work;
- test readings;
- recommendations;
- warranty claims;
- modifications;
- commissioning evidence; and
- replacement or removal.

A reliable history helps engineers avoid repeating diagnosis, allows managers to identify recurring faults and supports informed repair-versus-replacement decisions.

## Purchase requirement

A purchase requirement should identify:

- requesting person and accountable work owner;
- job, project, stock or overhead purpose;
- customer and site where relevant;
- description and quantity;
- manufacturer, model and part number where known;
- technical ratings and compatibility constraints;
- required date and delivery location;
- whether the item is for diagnosis, permanent repair, temporary repair or warranty remediation;
- acceptable alternatives and who may approve them;
- expected cost or budget basis;
- customer authority where cost depends on customer approval;
- Frostline expenditure authority;
- evidence-preservation or return requirements; and
- consequence of delay.

A photograph or informal message may support identification, but it does not replace a usable requirement.

## Requirement maturity

Procurement distinguishes between:

- **identification requested** — technical assistance is needed to determine the item;
- **price requested** — specification is sufficiently stable for supplier quotation;
- **approval requested** — price and terms are known but authority is outstanding;
- **ready to order** — specification, supplier, price, authority and delivery basis are confirmed;
- **ordered** — Frostline has issued a commitment;
- **supplier acknowledged** — the supplier has accepted scope, price and expected date;
- **dispatched**;
- **received**; and
- **technically accepted or quarantined**.

These states prevent “ordered” being used loosely to mean that somebody has merely asked a supplier a question.

## Diagnostic principles

Diagnosis aims to identify the verified cause of a problem, not merely the component that currently shows an alarm.

Engineers work from symptoms and evidence, considering:

- customer description;
- equipment state on arrival;
- alarms and fault history;
- operating conditions;
- electrical supply and controls demand;
- temperatures, pressures and flows;
- sensors and interlocks;
- previous work;
- environmental conditions; and
- manufacturer information.

Parts should not be replaced solely because they are associated with a fault code. A code may identify the detected condition rather than its root cause.

## Substitutions

A proposed substitute is reviewed for:

- duty and performance;
- dimensions and connections;
- electrical characteristics;
- refrigerant or medium compatibility;
- controls and communications;
- compliance and certification;
- installation method;
- maintenance and spare parts;
- warranty;
- customer specification;
- programme; and
- price and margin.

Technical approval, customer approval and commercial approval remain separate where each applies.

A supplier cannot approve equivalence on Frostline's behalf.

## Spare parts and supersession

Manufacturer support is judged partly by:

- accuracy of parts identification;
- stock availability;
- lead time;
- distribution network;
- technical documentation;
- supersession information;
- compatibility evidence; and
- support for older products.

A superseded part is not assumed equivalent solely because it replaces an old number in a catalogue. Frostline confirms any required wiring, controls, mounting, firmware or commissioning change.

A low equipment purchase price can be outweighed by poor parts availability over the asset life.

## Approved, conditional and restricted suppliers

Approved status is scoped. A supplier may be suitable for standard consumables but not specialist controls, refrigerants, safety-critical equipment or subcontract labour.

A supplier may be:

- approved;
- approved with category or value restrictions;
- approved only for emergency use;
- under performance review;
- suspended; or
- prohibited.

Use outside the normal approval scope requires explicit authority and recorded rationale.

## Parts and materials readiness

A part being requested, quoted, ordered, acknowledged, dispatched, delivered and available for use are different states.

Work requiring a specific item is not normally ready until Frostline knows:

- exact item or approved alternative;
- quantity and condition;
- current location;
- reservation or allocation;
- expected availability date;
- collection or delivery plan;
- associated consumables and tools; and
- whether return, warranty or evidence-preservation requirements apply.

The physical presence of a part does not prove that it is unallocated, technically suitable or commercially authorised for the job.

## Open purchasing exceptions

Active purchasing exceptions identify:

- exact state;
- work affected;
- operational and financial impact;
- supplier action expected;
- Frostline owner;
- next chase or decision date; and
- escalation route.

“Supplier issue” is not a meaningful status.

## Direct deliveries

Direct delivery to an engineer, customer site or project requires:

- named recipient;
- delivery location and access;
- expected date and time window;
- checking responsibility;
- proof of condition;
- storage plan;
- allocation reference; and
- escalation if nobody can accept the goods.

A carrier signature proves a delivery event, not necessarily correct specification, complete quantity or undamaged condition.

## Receipt and technical acceptance

Receipt confirms goods arrived. Technical acceptance may require later checks, unpacking, documentation review or engineer inspection.

Goods remain quarantined where they are:

- damaged;
- incorrect;
- incomplete;
- of uncertain provenance;
- missing certificates;
- electrically or mechanically suspect;
- potentially contaminated; or
- subject to a warranty evidence hold.

Quarantined goods are not included in available stock.

## Receipt exceptions

Where goods are damaged, incomplete, incorrect or undocumented, the receiver:

- separates them from available stock;
- records photographs and packaging condition where useful;
- links the purchase order and delivery evidence;
- notifies procurement;
- identifies operational impact;
- assigns the next action; and
- preserves return deadlines.

Quietly placing uncertain goods on a shelf creates false availability and weakens supplier recovery.

## Supplier errors and returns

Incorrect, damaged or surplus goods are protected from use and linked to:

- original purchase order;
- receipt evidence;
- photographs;
- supplier notification;
- return authority;
- carriage responsibility;
- replacement requirement;
- expected credit; and
- next-action owner.

Where Frostline ordered incorrectly, the cause and cost remain visible rather than being reclassified as supplier failure.

## Invoice matching

Supplier invoices are matched to:

- approved purchase commitment;
- supplier acknowledgement;
- receipt or service evidence;
- price and quantity;
- carriage and extras;
- returns and credits; and
- job, project, stock or overhead allocation.

A valid undisputed amount should not be delayed merely because another line is under review. Disputed and undisputed treatment is separated where practical.

## Supplier invoices

Purchase invoices are matched where practical to:

- approved supplier order;
- goods or service receipt;
- job, project, stock or overhead destination;
- agreed price and quantity;
- delivery and carriage;
- returns or shortages; and
- applicable VAT treatment.

Differences may arise from carriage, restocking, substitutions, partial deliveries, price changes or consolidated supplier billing.

Materials bought locally during urgent work may arrive through card receipts or engineer expenses rather than the normal purchase-order route. These still need evidence, authority and attribution to the correct job or overhead category.

Where an invoice cannot be matched, the exception identifies the disputed element, operational owner, supplier action and payment decision. Finance should not guess technical equivalence or receipt from a vague description.

## Accruals and uninvoiced cost

Work may consume goods or services before the supplier invoice arrives. Material uninvoiced cost is accrued or forecast so project and period performance are not overstated.

Sources include:

- supplier goods received but not invoiced;
- subcontract attendance completed;
- access or hire still running;
- estimated utility or disposal charges;
- engineer expenses not yet submitted; and
- warranty replacements expected to become chargeable if recovery fails.

Accruals are reviewed and released when actual invoices, credits or final decisions arrive.

## Available, reserved and quarantined states

Frostline distinguishes:

- **available** — suitable and uncommitted for authorised issue;
- **reserved** — committed to a named job, project, customer or purpose;
- **picked** — removed from normal storage for imminent issue;
- **issued** — custody transferred to an engineer, van, site or project;
- **quarantined** — not permitted for use pending decision;
- **return pending** — controlled for supplier or manufacturer return;
- **evidence hold** — preserved for technical, warranty, insurance or dispute reasons;
- **obsolete** — no longer suitable for normal use; and
- **disposed** — removed under authorised disposal control.

One generic “in stock” status is insufficient.

## Customer-owned and consignment stock

Customer-owned, consignment or manufacturer-loaned goods remain distinct from Frostline-owned inventory.

Records identify:

- owner;
- agreement or purpose;
- custody location;
- permitted use;
- insurance or loss position;
- replenishment responsibility; and
- return conditions.

Physical possession does not transfer ownership.

## Van stock and distributed inventory

Van stock can support first-time completion but remains part of controlled inventory. Planning may consider:

- which engineer carries likely items;
- whether recorded stock is physically credible;
- whether an item is reserved or already consumed;
- transfer time between engineers;
- replenishment consequences; and
- whether using scarce stock would disadvantage a higher-priority need.

Informal promises such as “Dave has one somewhere” are treated as unconfirmed until verified.

## Picking and issue

Picking confirms that the intended item, quantity and condition have been selected for the correct work.

Issue records may include:

- job or project;
- engineer or recipient;
- van or site destination;
- item and quantity;
- serial or batch where relevant;
- date and time;
- source location;
- whether the issue is chargeable, included, warranty-related or temporary; and
- required return of unused or failed material.

Physical custody and financial allocation are related but not identical. A part can be issued before its final warranty or customer-charge treatment is known.

## Reservation and allocation

A reservation identifies:

- item and quantity;
- intended job, project, contract or customer;
- requester;
- accountable work owner;
- required date;
- reservation date;
- review or expiry date;
- substitution permission; and
- consequence if reallocated.

Reserved stock is not reassigned merely because another job is urgent. Reallocation requires review of both commitments and notification to the displaced owner.

Long-standing reservations are reviewed so cancelled, delayed or superseded work does not lock useful inventory indefinitely.

## Cancellation and surplus commitments

When work changes or is cancelled, procurement reviews:

- whether the supplier order can be cancelled;
- cancellation or restocking cost;
- ownership of made-to-order goods;
- alternative use;
- customer recovery entitlement;
- storage and obsolescence risk; and
- financial provision required.

The operational job may be cancelled while the purchasing exposure remains active.

## Returns from engineers and sites

Returned items are assessed before re-entry to available stock.

The assessment considers whether the item is:

- unused and sealed;
- opened but complete;
- damaged;
- contaminated;
- electrically or mechanically suspect;
- missing accessories;
- customer-owned;
- removed from service;
- subject to supplier return;
- warranty evidence; or
- suitable only for disposal.

A returned item is not automatically reusable merely because it looks intact.

## Credit approval and exposure

New customers and material increases in exposure may require credit checks, deposits, pro-forma payment or director approval.

Credit exposure includes more than issued invoices. Frostline considers:

- overdue and not-yet-due debt;
- uninvoiced completed work;
- work in progress;
- committed materials and subcontract cost;
- accepted but not yet started work;
- retention;
- disputed value;
- pending credits; and
- obligations that cannot readily be cancelled.

A customer can appear within its credit limit while Frostline has substantial unbilled commitment. Significant release decisions therefore use current and forecast exposure, not ledger balance alone.

## Obsolete and slow-moving stock

Stock may become obsolete because of:

- equipment-range change;
- part supersession;
- refrigerant transition;
- shelf-life expiry;
- damage or contamination;
- lost customer base;
- changed regulation;
- unsupported controls platform; or
- project cancellation.

Review considers return, redeployment, sale, component recovery where safe, controlled retention, write-down or disposal.

Past purchase cost is not evidence of current value.

## Core principle

A report that recently supplied or worked-on equipment has failed creates a **suspected warranty case**, not an automatic conclusion that Frostline must absorb every resulting cost.

The business must distinguish:

1. whether a defect or failure exists;
2. the verified or likely cause;
3. whether immediate action is needed to make safe or restore service;
4. which party is contractually responsible;
5. which costs are recoverable and from whom; and
6. what evidence is needed to close the case.

Customer care may justify rapid attendance before responsibility is known. That service decision must remain visible rather than silently becoming an admission of liability.

## Warranty records

Frostline retains distinct but linked records for:

- the original quotation, contract or project;
- the installed or serviced asset;
- commissioning and completion evidence;
- the customer's defect report;
- the suspected warranty case;
- each diagnostic or remedial job;
- manufacturer or supplier claim references;
- costs incurred and recoveries received;
- technical conclusions; and
- final responsibility decision.

A remedial job can be operationally complete while the warranty case remains financially unresolved.

## Cost control

Warranty cost includes all directly attributable value, including:

- engineer and management time;
- travel;
- replacement parts and freight;
- refrigerant and consumables;
- access equipment;
- subcontractors;
- temporary equipment;
- disposal and return charges;
- credits issued; and
- unrecovered manufacturer or supplier claims.

Costs are recorded even where Frostline deliberately absorbs them. Zero-price customer work is not zero-cost work.

Recoveries are recorded separately from costs so that gross exposure, recovery performance and final net cost remain visible.

## Labour and warranty recovery

Manufacturer warranty may cover replacement parts without covering all Frostline labour, travel, access equipment, refrigerant, freight or consequential work. The customer contract and manufacturer terms determine how cost is treated.

Frostline records separately:

- gross cost incurred;
- customer charge;
- manufacturer claim value;
- supplier or distributor credit;
- subcontract recovery;
- goodwill contribution;
- amount received; and
- final unrecovered cost.

A promised replacement or credit is not treated as recovered value until confirmed and processed.

## Warranty and evidence custody

Removed components linked to suspected warranty, product failure, insurance or technical investigation are controlled through chain of custody.

The record should identify:

- source customer, site, system and asset;
- related job and warranty case;
- date removed;
- engineer;
- reported and observed failure;
- photographs or test references;
- condition and packaging;
- storage location;
- return or inspection deadline;
- manufacturer or supplier reference; and
- person owning the next action.

Evidence items are not stripped for parts, scrapped or mixed with general returns until authorised.

## Closure criteria

A warranty case closes only when:

- immediate safety and operational actions are complete;
- the affected job or project records are linked;
- technical findings are recorded at an appropriate level of confidence;
- responsibility has been decided or the unresolved position explicitly accepted;
- customer communication is complete;
- remedial work is tested;
- supplier, manufacturer or subcontract recoveries are resolved or written off under authority;
- customer charges, credits or goodwill decisions are completed; and
- learning actions have owners where required.

Closing the remedial job is not sufficient if the recovery, dispute or root-cause action remains ownerless.

## Customer communication

Customers receive clear updates covering:

- current operational condition;
- immediate safety position;
- tests or remedial work completed;
- temporary limitations;
- what remains uncertain;
- next action and owner;
- expected response or review point; and
- current commercial position where known.

Technical uncertainty is expressed honestly. Frostline does not imply confirmed manufacturer acceptance before a claim is approved.

## Claim authority

A manufacturer may require prior approval before parts are changed, removed or returned. Frostline records whether approval covers:

- diagnosis only;
- replacement part supply;
- field labour;
- travel;
- refrigerant or consumables;
- access equipment;
- return carriage;
- specialist attendance; or
- a maximum authorised value.

A claim number alone is not proof that every cost is recoverable. Work outside the stated authorisation requires separate Frostline and customer decisions.

## Customer-chargeable outcomes

An attendance may become customer chargeable where evidence supports causes such as:

- no defect in Frostline-supplied work;
- customer misuse or unauthorised alteration;
- missed required maintenance;
- external power, controls, water, drainage or environmental conditions;
- consumable replacement;
- accidental damage;
- work outside warranty scope or period; or
- unrelated failure.

The decision to convert a warranty investigation to a customer charge requires three inputs: the engineer's technical conclusion (evidence that the cause is not a Frostline or supplier defect), an operational or account manager's confirmation that the customer should be informed and invoiced, and finance's agreement that the commercial basis and evidence are sufficient to raise the charge. No single role completes this decision alone.

Chargeability is communicated with evidence and reference to applicable terms. Frostline avoids surprising the customer with an unexplained invoice after repeatedly calling the case “warranty”.

Where the commercial position is sensitive, account management may choose a goodwill contribution. Goodwill is an explicit business decision and does not rewrite technical responsibility.

### Warranty decisions

Warranty treatment requires a separate conclusion about:

- whether a defect exists;
- whether it falls within Frostline workmanship responsibility, manufacturer warranty, subcontract responsibility or customer charge;
- whether exclusions apply;
- who authorises remedial attendance; and
- who ultimately bears the cost.

Calling work “warranty” before responsibility is established can conceal cost and weaken recovery from the correct party.

## Disputes

Invoice disputes often require input from engineers, coordinators, estimators, project managers or account owners. Finance records the disputed amount and reason but does not invent the operational answer.

A useful dispute record includes:

- invoice and disputed line or amount;
- customer's stated reason;
- accepted scope and authority;
- delivery evidence;
- chronology;
- relevant correspondence;
- Frostline technical and commercial position;
- owner of the response;
- next action and due date; and
- proposed outcome or escalation.

Resolution may involve additional evidence, correction of administrative details, commercial negotiation, partial credit, variation agreement or firm enforcement of the original charge.

The undisputed portion should remain separately visible and collectible where appropriate.

## Credits, write-offs and goodwill

Frostline distinguishes:

- correction of an incorrect invoice;
- contractual credit;
- service-recovery goodwill;
- warranty responsibility;
- bad-debt write-off;
- supplier credit; and
- accounting adjustment.

A customer credit note changes the financial record but does not delete the original operational history or technical conclusion.

Material credits and write-offs require a recorded reason and delegated approval. Goodwill is a commercial decision; it must not be used to disguise recurring delivery defects or rewrite responsibility.

## Product recalls and safety notices

Manufacturers may issue recalls, safety notices or mandatory modifications. Frostline assesses:

- authenticity and scope of the notice;
- affected product, model and serial population;
- customers, sites and assets potentially affected;
- risk level and required timescale;
- interim operating restrictions;
- parts and labour arrangements;
- communication responsibility;
- access and scheduling dependencies;
- commercial recovery; and
- evidence of completion.

Urgent safety action is prioritised even where commercial responsibility is still being resolved.

A recall campaign is controlled as a portfolio of linked affected assets and actions, not a loose collection of emails.

## Unsafe or restricted systems

Where equipment cannot safely remain in normal operation, the engineer controls it within competence and authority through isolation, warning, restricted use or escalation.

The record states:

- hazard;
- control applied;
- residual risk;
- who was informed;
- whether the customer accepted custody of an isolated system; and
- next required action.

Customer objection to downtime does not authorise unsafe operation.

## Calibration

Calibration provides confidence that an instrument's readings are acceptably related to recognised standards. Not every tool requires formal calibration, but instruments used for safety, commissioning, compliance or technical evidence may require controlled verification.

Calibration records may include:

- asset and serial number;
- calibration date;
- result and certificate;
- due date;
- range or limitations;
- repair or adjustment; and
- assigned engineer.

An in-date label does not replace the certificate or condition check.

## Competence records

Records normally include:

- qualifications and certificates;
- expiry or renewal dates;
- manufacturer courses;
- equipment familiarisation;
- internal assessments;
- supervised-work evidence;
- restrictions or development needs;
- toolbox talks attended; and
- role-specific authorisations.

Records must distinguish attendance from successful assessment and current competence from historical exposure.

## What counts as a complaint

A complaint is any clear expression that Frostline's service, conduct, work, communication or commercial treatment was unacceptable. It does not need to use the word "complaint" or arrive through a formal channel.

Common subjects include:

- missed or late attendance;
- failure to provide updates;
- repeat breakdowns;
- disputed diagnosis or workmanship;
- damage or cleanliness concerns;
- engineer behaviour;
- unexpected charges;
- invoice discrepancies;
- failure to meet a contractual commitment; and
- dissatisfaction with the proposed remedy.

## Incident response

Following an incident, priorities are:

1. protect life and prevent further harm;
2. make the site safe within competence and authority;
3. contact emergency services or specialists where required;
4. preserve evidence;
5. notify management;
6. meet regulatory or contractual reporting duties; and
7. notify insurers or brokers within policy timescales.

Employees should not speculate about blame or promise compensation at the scene.

## Service recovery

Possible remedies include:

- explanation or apology;
- urgent reattendance;
- corrective work;
- independent inspection;
- replacement parts or equipment;
- invoice correction;
- credit or refund;
- contribution toward reasonable loss;
- revised operating guidance; and
- termination of the relationship where trust cannot be restored.

The remedy should address the actual failure. A small credit is not an adequate substitute for unresolved unsafe workmanship, and repeated free visits may not solve a fundamentally unsuitable system.

## Severity and escalation

Higher-level escalation is appropriate where there is:

- actual or potential injury;
- gas, electrical, pressure or refrigerant safety risk;
- significant property damage;
- allegation of dishonesty, discrimination or serious misconduct;
- regulatory involvement;
- likely legal action;
- substantial financial exposure;
- major customer relationship risk; or
- evidence of a repeated systemic failure.

Insurers, legal advisers, manufacturers or regulators may need to be notified. Notifications should not be delayed merely to complete an internal investigation.

## Closure

A complaint is closed when:

- the findings have been communicated;
- agreed corrective actions are complete or scheduled;
- financial adjustments are authorised;
- any safety or compliance actions are complete;
- the customer's response is recorded; and
- learning actions have owners.

A customer does not have to agree with the outcome for the complaint to be closed, but Frostline must be able to explain the basis of its decision.

## First-time fix

A first-time fix is desirable but not always realistic. It is more likely when:

- the fault description is accurate;
- asset information is available;
- the engineer has the right skills;
- common parts are held in van stock;
- access is available;
- the repair is within scope and competence; and
- both customer and Frostline commercial authority exist.

For reporting, first-time fix means that the suitable reactive issue was durably restored or resolved during the first attendance without an unplanned further engineering attendance. It excludes cases where return work was always planned, where investigation was the authorised scope, or where no safe and justified repair could reasonably be expected.

Engineers must not fit speculative parts merely to improve the measure.

## Performance measurement

Reporting may include:

- acknowledgement attainment;
- initial-response attainment;
- attendance attainment;
- restoration attainment;
- resolution attainment where applicable;
- breaches by priority and cause;
- clock-stop frequency and duration;
- priority changes;
- update compliance;
- repeat incidents; and
- unresolved incidents by age and dependency.

Every measure states denominator, exclusions, time basis and source event. Averages are supplemented by percentiles, breach counts and significant-case narrative.

## Data quality

Common data-quality problems include:

- inconsistent job closure;
- missing labour or materials;
- duplicate customer or asset records;
- inaccurate priority codes;
- open jobs without next actions;
- delayed invoice status;
- changed KPI definitions; and
- manual adjustments without explanation.

Poor data is identified rather than silently converted into confident reporting.

## Qualification

Frostline does not pursue every enquiry automatically. Initial qualification considers:

- location and travel implications;
- equipment and service type;
- required accreditations or technical capability;
- likely contract value;
- timescale;
- customer payment risk;
- access and working-hour constraints;
- whether the opportunity fits Frostline's commercial model; and
- whether capacity exists to deliver responsibly.

Declining unsuitable work early is preferable to accepting work that cannot be delivered safely, profitably or to the expected standard.

## Opportunity qualification

Before investing significant estimating effort, Frostline considers:

- customer fit and payment history;
- location and delivery capacity;
- technical competence required;
- timescale and access constraints;
- likelihood of award;
- tender requirements;
- project value and risk; and
- whether the opportunity matches Frostline's services.

Some enquiries are declined where the scope is unsuitable, information is inadequate or the requested programme cannot be responsibly supported.

Qualification is a decision to pursue, not a promise to quote and not customer authority to begin work. The opportunity record identifies its owner, next action, decision date and any information still required.

## Geographic coverage

Targets reflect realistic travel, engineer distribution, site access and competence. Geographic zones may have different commitments.

Attendance risk is affected by:

- travel from actual engineer locations;
- congestion and road closures;
- parking and security;
- rural or remote access;
- weather;
- specialist competence availability; and
- whether travel occurs inside or outside normal hours.

The company avoids selling response commitments based only on depot-to-postcode distance.

## Sales conduct

Frostline does not:

- invent urgency;
- disparage competitors without evidence;
- promise unavailable engineers or parts;
- misrepresent accreditation or manufacturer status;
- hide exclusions likely to affect the decision; or
- use technical complexity to pressure a customer.

Sustainable growth depends on work that Frostline can deliver well, collect payment for and support after completion.

## Energy performance

Energy performance depends on more than nominal equipment efficiency. Actual consumption is affected by:

- correct sizing;
- building fabric and internal gains;
- controls and set points;
- operating hours;
- simultaneous heating and cooling;
- maintenance condition;
- airflow and water flow;
- refrigerant charge;
- sensor accuracy;
- user behaviour; and
- weather.

Frostline avoids claiming guaranteed savings without a credible baseline, defined assumptions and a way to measure the outcome.

## Approved installer programmes

Some manufacturers operate approved, accredited or partner installer schemes. Membership may require:

- technical training;
- minimum installation standards;
- evidence of insurance and certification;
- warranty registration performance;
- use of approved commissioning procedures;
- periodic assessment;
- minimum sales or purchase levels; and
- compliance with brand rules.

Programme status may provide extended warranty options, technical access, sales support or customer referrals. It should not be presented as independent proof that a product is suitable for every site or that every Frostline engineer is authorised on every product.

Programme conditions, renewal dates, engineer-level restrictions and consequences of lapse are retained where they affect customer commitments.

## Sales stages

An opportunity may progress through:

1. identified;
2. qualified;
3. surveyed or investigated;
4. solution developed;
5. quotation or tender submitted;
6. clarification or negotiation;
7. won, lost, deferred or withdrawn; and
8. handed into delivery.

Stages should reflect reality. A quotation sent without confirmed funding or authority is not necessarily close to award.

## Advice records and authority

Material manufacturer advice is recorded with:

- support case or reference;
- person or team providing advice;
- date and communication route;
- evidence supplied;
- advice received;
- conditions or caveats;
- tests or actions authorised;
- parts or software identified; and
- next action and owner.

The engineer on site remains responsible for local safety and must not follow advice they reasonably believe is unsafe or outside competence.

Manufacturer advice can inform a technical decision. It does not by itself:

- authorise customer expenditure;
- authorise Frostline purchasing;
- amend a contract;
- approve a variation;
- admit liability; or
- guarantee reimbursement.

## Software and firmware

Modern controls and connected equipment may depend on software or firmware. Updates can correct faults, add compatibility or address security concerns, but may also alter behaviour.

Before a significant update, Frostline considers:

- reason and authority;
- current and target version;
- release notes and known issues;
- configuration backup;
- controls and network dependencies;
- downtime and rollback;
- recommissioning requirements;
- customer or controls-contractor involvement; and
- warranty or support implications.

The update and resulting tests are recorded. An undocumented firmware change can make future diagnosis and responsibility assessment materially harder.

## Controls and BMS interfaces

HVAC faults often involve the boundary between mechanical equipment, local controls and a building-management system.

Checks may include:

- enable signals;
- schedules and occupancy modes;
- set points and dead bands;
- sensor values;
- interlocks;
- network communication;
- actuator commands and feedback;
- alarm routing;
- overrides; and
- responsibility boundaries.

A unit that does not run may be operating correctly in response to an absent enable. Equally, a BMS alarm may be caused by a local equipment fault.

Frostline avoids altering customer controls without understanding the wider sequence and obtaining appropriate authority.

### Technical decisions

Technical decisions establish what is known, what remains uncertain, what tests are justified and what remedy is recommended.

Engineers may normally:

- diagnose within their competence;
- select routine methods and materials within an authorised scope;
- reject an unsafe or technically unsuitable instruction;
- recommend repair, replacement, monitoring or further investigation; and
- request technical escalation.

They may not represent an unverified diagnosis as fact, alter a materially different system without review, or commit the customer to unapproved follow-on work.

## Escalation and relationship ownership

Frostline maintains named sales, technical, warranty and service contacts where possible. Significant unresolved issues are escalated with:

- a clear question or required outcome;
- chronology;
- supporting evidence;
- customer and operational impact;
- cost exposure;
- previous responses;
- required decision date; and
- Frostline owner.

A friendly relationship is useful, but it is not a control. Unresolved manufacturer actions remain visible until accepted, concluded, transferred or deliberately written off under authority.

## Conflicts and escalation

Common conflicts include:

- a customer demanding unsafe work;
- an engineer recommending work beyond the authorised budget;
- a coordinator needing to displace another contractual commitment;
- a manager wanting work completed before a purchase order is received;
- finance placing a customer on stop while operations considers the site critical; and
- a manufacturer declining warranty where Frostline believes the failure is covered.

The correct response is explicit escalation, not silent assumption. Until resolved, the record should show the current constraint and the owner of the next decision.
